From: MediaBox
Subject: POLLIN means a decoded frame is waiting, not that a packet came back

The plugin has one signal to give a client: the descriptor is readable, or it
is not. It raises it for three different things -- a pending event, a buffer
returning on the OUTPUT queue, and a frame ready on the CAPTURE queue.

Only the last of those is POLLIN. In V4L2, POLLIN on a memory-to-memory device
means one thing: a buffer can be dequeued from the CAPTURE queue. A returning
OUTPUT buffer is the other direction and is POLLOUT, which this descriptor
cannot express anyway.

Chromium takes POLLIN at its word, and it is fed compressed data long before
the stream's resolution is known -- so it is told there is a decoded frame
waiting while its CAPTURE queue is still a null pointer:

    void V4L2StatefulVideoDecoder::TryAndDequeueCAPTUREQueueBuffers() {
      DCHECK(CAPTURE_queue_) << "|CAPTURE_queue_| must be created at this point";
      const v4l2_memory queue_type = CAPTURE_queue_->GetMemoryType();

DCHECK is compiled out of every shipping build, so the second line dereferences
null. Measured on RK3588: within about two seconds of the first packet the
browser logs "GPU process exited unexpectedly: exit_code=11" twice, the decoder
is abandoned, and playback continues in software for every codec -- H.264 and
AV1 alike. Nothing in the browser's log connects the crash to the decoder; from
the outside it reads as "video decoder fallback after initial decode error".

So the OUTPUT term goes. The client loses nothing by it: it drains its own
OUTPUT queue before refilling it, from the thread that feeds the decoder, and
never waits on poll() for permission to enqueue.
--- a/src/libv4l-rkmpp.c
+++ b/src/libv4l-rkmpp.c
@@ -871,7 +871,27 @@
 	else
 		has_event = rkmpp_enc_has_event(ctx->data);
 
-	has_event |= !TAILQ_EMPTY(&ctx->output.avail_buffers);
+	/*
+	 * A buffer coming back on the OUTPUT queue is not POLLIN.
+	 *
+	 * This descriptor carries exactly one signal -- readable, or not -- and
+	 * a V4L2 client reads readable as POLLIN, which in V4L2 means one thing
+	 * only: a buffer can be dequeued from the CAPTURE queue. Raising it for
+	 * a returning OUTPUT buffer tells the client there is a decoded frame
+	 * waiting before the CAPTURE queue exists at all.
+	 *
+	 * Chromium takes that literally. Its stateful decoder answers POLLIN by
+	 * calling TryAndDequeueCAPTUREQueueBuffers(), which is guarded only by a
+	 * DCHECK -- compiled out of every shipping build -- and then dereferences
+	 * a CAPTURE queue that is still null. Measured on RK3588: the GPU process
+	 * dies with SIGSEGV within two seconds of the first frame being fed, the
+	 * browser falls back to software for every codec, and nothing in its log
+	 * connects the two.
+	 *
+	 * The client does not lose anything. It drains the OUTPUT queue itself
+	 * before refilling it, on its own thread, and never waits on poll() to
+	 * be told it may enqueue more.
+	 */
 	has_event |= !TAILQ_EMPTY(&ctx->capture.avail_buffers);
 
 	/* Report POLLIN event */
